.TH CONFMASK 1 "2026" "confmask 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
confmask \- run a process tree with a redacted /proc entry
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B confmask
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fB\-\-\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR...]
.br
.B confmask
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fB\-\-args\-from\-fd\fR \fIN\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B confmask
is a ptrace supervisor.
It runs a program and every process that program spawns.
Each process shows a presentation label in
.BR /proc/ pid /cmdline
and
.BR /proc/ pid /comm
instead of its real command line and process name.
Every other local account sees the label.
The program itself receives its real arguments and environment.
.PP
A process is syscall-supervised until its first exec completes.
At the execve entry stop the supervisor rewrites the argument and
environment pointer arrays of the old image.
The kernel then copies the label and empty strings, so the real command
line never exists in the new image.
At the exec completion stop the supervisor writes the real strings into
scratch space below the new stack pointer and repoints the arrays.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-label " \fINAME\fR"
Presentation label.
The value
.B \-
gives a blank label (one space).
The label must not contain NUL, LF or CR.
Default: the program name, with all arguments removed.
.TP
.B \-\-label\-empty
Empty label.
.TP
.BI \-\-argv0 " NAME"
argv[0] of the program.
Default: the command exactly as typed.
.TP
.BI \-\-comm " NAME"
Process name for
.BR top ,
at most 15 bytes.
Default: the label cut to 15 bytes.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-env
Leave
.B /proc/\fIpid\fB/environ
visible.
.TP
.BI \-\-keep\-env= NAME[,NAME...]
Keep only the named variables visible.
The inline form is required.
Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-env " K=V"
Add or replace one variable.
Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-env\-file " PATH"
Read variables from a file: one KEY=VALUE per line, # starts a comment
line, blank lines are skipped, values are taken as written.
Repeatable.
.TP
.BI \-\-args\-from\-fd " N"
Read the command from file descriptor N as a NUL-separated vector:
program first, then arguments.
.TP
.BI \-\-on\-exit " POLICY"
What happens to descendants still running when the command exits:
.B detach
(default; each settled descendant is detached and keeps the label),
.B wait
(supervise until the whole tree exits) or
.B kill
(send SIGKILL).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-follow
Redact only the direct child.
Descendants are traced but not redacted.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-exit\-kill
Do not set PTRACE_O_EXITKILL: the tree survives if the supervisor dies.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-dumpable
Set PR_SET_DUMPABLE=0 in every process of the tree.
Same-uid observers then cannot read environ, maps, exe or fd.
Core dumps and debugger attach stop working.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-new\-privs
Set no_new_privs before exec.
Set-uid and file-capability programs gain nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-watch\-rename
Revert a later prctl(PR_SET_NAME) to the label.
Installs a seccomp filter that traps that call only.
Implies
.BR \-\-no\-new\-privs .
.TP
.B \-\-rexec
Exec the program from a sealed memfd, so
.B /proc/\fIpid\fB/exe
names the label.
.TP
.B \-\-unshare
Run in new user, pid and mount namespaces with a private /proc, when
the kernel allows it.
Prints one line and continues without it otherwise.
.TP
.BI \-\-scope\-name " NAME"
Re-exec once through
.BR "systemd-run \-\-scope" .
The unit name is visible in
.BR /proc/\fIpid\fB/cgroup .
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress diagnostics, including the session summary.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Trace every redaction event to stderr.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the help text.
.SH ALIASES
.BR \-a " (= \-\-label), " \-a\-
(= \-l\-),
.BR \-E " (= \-\-keep\-env), " \-F " (= \-\-no\-follow), " \-K " (= \-\-no\-exit\-kill)."
.B \-f
and
.B \-c
are accepted and have no effect.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B CONFMASK_NO_SELFMASK
Do not redact the supervisor's own /proc entry.
Use only for debugging: the supervisor then shows its real command line,
which contains the secrets it was given.
.SH LABEL LIMITS
The label is cut at three places.
.B comm
keeps 15 bytes.
The exec entry path keeps 254 bytes.
The post-exec path cuts the label to the span of the argument area.
A warning is printed once when the label is longer than 254 bytes.
.SH SESSION SUMMARY
When any exec ran with less than the full entry-swap redaction, the
session prints one line:
.PP
.RS
confmask: \fIN\fR of \fIM\fR execs unredacted (\fIreasons\fR)
.RE
.PP
The reasons are
.BR script ,
.BR reexec ,
.BR unredacted ,
.B comm
and
.BR foreign\-arch .
.B \-\-quiet
suppresses the line.
.SH DAEMON MODE
With
.BI \-\-daemon " PATH"
the job runs inside a
.BR confmaskd (8).
The daemon owns the environment and the masking policy.
Mask options other than
.B \-\-label
and
.B \-\-label\-empty
have no effect there and produce a warning.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit status of the supervised command.
A command killed by a signal makes
.B confmask
die by the same signal.
Usage errors exit 255.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR confmaskd (8)
.SH LICENSE
0BSD.
